Let's outline episodes:

1. A traveler arrives at a workshop where a bridge is being built. The master's seat is empty; apprentices continue because the pattern hangs on the wall. "No seat load-bearing": chair remains empty; work continues from pattern; any hand can take tools. (Law1)

2. Ledger-keeper records a mistake: draws a line through? No, adds a correcting entry; old line stays. "Deletion is theft": record grows; wrong superseded. (Law2)

3. Presence not authority: during a collapse, an injured apprentice's name is written as hurt; a man in the room is not blamed. Maybe "The injured boy's name went into the ledger under harm; the man beside him went in under presence, and no one wrote his hand as cause." (Law3)

4. Map vs floor: surveyor's map shows straight bank; actual riverbank curved; builders trust ground, redraw map. (Law4)

5. Walls are way: old workshop walls have angles; each angle from a flood limit; reading walls reveals plan. (Law5)

6. Sow never lands in own store: At sowing, seed must leave hand; if it falls back into sower's pouch, not counted. Could be "grain for the bridge's mortar"? Hmm.

7. Kill condition: Before testing a beam, builder says "if it bends more than X, it fails"; license to speak. (Law7)

8. Real world vs nodding: Committee praises a stone; flood washes it away; another stone endures; character returns under real world. (Law8)

9. One key one lock: A gate has two keys that both turn but door stays shut; "two readings both fit, door broken." Could be a lock on ledger? Need.

10. One sense one instance: Each worker declares what they cannot see; no one holds another's cards. Maybe during inspection, each says "I cannot see the far joint"; no one speaks for another. (Law10)

