Draft:

THE BOOK

A traveler came to a river town where a bridge was being raised. In the workshop, the master's chair stood empty, yet the apprentices worked from the pattern pinned to the wall, and any hand that knew the weave could take up the shuttle. The ledger-keeper sat by the door and wrote what happened; when he mistook a length, he did not scratch it out but added a new line beneath it, so the page grew and the first mark stayed as a superseded truth.

One morning a beam slipped and struck a boy. The ledger named the boy under hurt and named the men in the room under present; it did not say whose hand had let go, because the room only proved they were in it. The surveyor brought a map that showed a straight bank, but the ground at the water's edge curved; the builders trusted the mud and redrew the map before laying stone.

The workshop itself was old, and its walls met at strange angles. Each angle had been cut by a flood mark or a property line, and a young mason learned that if he read those limits he had read the plan. When seed was sown for the riverbank grass,